#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605} :activity/id #uuid "cac9bb11-d48e-5140-9653-cc117881f57c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176968 :tutorial/content "Under what conditions can we be sure that the global extrema of a function exist?\n\nThe **extreme value theorem** answers this question and states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function defined on the closed interval $[a,b],$ then $f(x)$ always has at least one global maximum and one global minimum on $[a,b]$*.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5].\n$$\n\nNotice that this function satisfies the conditions for the extreme value theorem:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ is *continuous* on the interval $x\\in [2,5]$, and\n\n- the interval $[2,5]$ is *closed*.\n\nSince *both* conditions are satisfied, the extreme value theorem guarantees that $f(x)$ must have at least one global maximum and one global minimum.\n\nTo see that this is indeed true, let's sketch the graph of $f(x).$ \n\n![](images/d2/d2ceffc40ead80b03d73004b663b91a942444bd14ce9e1e68fc98ffebbae3423.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the function $f(x)$ has \n\n- a global maximum at $x=5,$ and\n\n- a global minimum at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "300a3bb8-a79f-5387-8c5e-1e403c2df531" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ff3fa5a-39c6-574d-bfa0-7e467bf44083" :step/math-academy-id 12791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175768 :question/id #uuid "59426214-0bb1-5e8b-9e96-0fb84ec6361c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508954}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8c5c661d-87d1-5fdd-b4cf-9b1217993d69" :step/math-academy-id 12792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175769 :question/id #uuid "2d31d7a1-bd6a-5794-a4af-b6cdad4dc64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341981}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "3001b2d1-c302-5b15-b3a6-3ff0fa36821e" :step/math-academy-id 12794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175770 :question/id #uuid "1d90dec2-3c79-5069-ba1b-7e7e3f16ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7889a404-286a-5541-b477-ed0966b909de" :step/math-academy-id 12795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177036 :tutorial/content "We can only apply the extreme value theorem to *continuous* functions defined on *closed* intervals. Both conditions are crucial.\n\nTo see why this is the case, consider the function $g(x),$ given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=(x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5).\n$$\n\nNotice that $g(x)$ is defined on a semi-open interval (the right endpoint is open). As a result, we cannot guarantee that $g(x)$ has a global maximum and a global minimum.\n\n![](images/a7/a7648d89fc02372f8d48a649425fa5dba4c8a86e12e9fa7bbddc69ffe52575c3.png)\n\nSimilarly, consider the function $h(x),$ given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}, & \\,2\\le x<3, \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+1, & \\,3\\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ is defined on the closed interval $[2,5].$ However, since $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[2,5],$ we cannot guarantee that $h(x)$ has both a global maximum and minimum on $[2,5].$\n\n![](images/b1/b1bdc0a3c384e5918bdac46ce5db31c0836069eb8a3383febf2aac86654929ea.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "08bf8574-c1f7-5521-983b-282f7b0c3cc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5745 :tutorial/title "Conditions for Applying the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fa2543e5-fe3a-57fe-a964-118b017f51bc" :step/math-academy-id 35242 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176121}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509010} :activity/id #uuid "b940a17e-dd53-53d8-bf19-3b3ed1a866fe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2fb7032-76c6-5840-a4b3-75b3bca0c64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468022} {:answer/id #uuid "7da58ced-39e0-5469-8345-6d5c5c71fd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468022} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5aeadf-4637-56d4-8985-6b916361fe7c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2b8f35-5eb0-5d60-99e3-d78e15538715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468024} {:answer/id #uuid "87c1ca6e-9c20-5182-95f4-352e35d95fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468024} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b25706e-95ef-5012-a511-a60525ecadd8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4850f6e7-44e4-5c2c-ab1c-1fd4345196e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468026} {:answer/id #uuid "5d89fdc2-6b87-505d-9741-6111f42f5c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468027}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468027} :answer-field/id #uuid "bafe51e4-c08a-55a8-b1fd-0a9ee0fb0ef6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5aba1141-c0a4-54c1-bd64-30eb78786676" :question/math-academy-id "q-73484" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}?$    \n\nI. $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$  is closed, but the function is not defined on a part of the interval because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$    is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "0ea7f73f-9a2c-5b1b-92bd-1284bb0cf34e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc37e5f-628a-5453-b9aa-5f7736bbc9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467590} {:answer/id #uuid "86c33542-1a37-5a8c-829b-f0ce4c00b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c6904f-d234-579f-a1ce-7aceea241e6b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b766daf9-f662-5b41-bf41-146b6ff13df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467592} {:answer/id #uuid "ffeccce2-717e-5e80-b3c0-db1259995cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467593} :answer-field/id #uuid "814f8eea-219e-55ac-a376-98a938bdbd22" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468517} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7babf862-7892-5b69-9b8c-2026a9d0a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467594} {:answer/id #uuid "61ed1c67-3d18-527d-aa8b-5e81455211be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467594} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cc6ec8-9bed-5ab7-9e0d-2ce973417c1a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a410e5f1-db4f-5d99-8a2f-cd16595bdd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-47923" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $[0.5,1]$\n- **III.** $[-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "47653334-ddc8-59af-8924-8f0b31ddb36e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08258f8b-dcaa-5fc6-ae65-f632b2ec32c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468052} {:answer/id #uuid "29791dee-07cd-517e-ade1-ff8025ea2d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468052} :answer-field/id #uuid "e380e8d8-76cf-5f78-bbc9-d5f2bfff507e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fa6caaf-0d53-550a-81a3-65d8c87e7671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468054} {:answer/id #uuid "7b92b7c9-5e55-5053-bc30-ae2124f51f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468054} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7306f5-05ce-59d5-926c-66b49d14119f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ffb37d-c85f-5070-8a42-00349cde98df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468056} {:answer/id #uuid "332ea8dc-cd67-5380-9b7b-ad3430d95bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468057} :answer-field/id #uuid "3acf4f19-1ed8-5841-bd74-4e01d898c416" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7a761269-f6ea-50eb-8e45-58983c3d9692" :question/math-academy-id "q-73503" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $(0,3]$\n- **III.** $[-2,-1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "98d94b5f-1594-5a4d-b5ff-4c0b24e893f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb3bff0-d429-5165-9782-7f65936a7d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508480} {:answer/id #uuid "690abb71-4d77-510e-a85b-3cd428ff1d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508548} {:answer/id #uuid "7f70533b-31ac-541b-be61-eba3464f5b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508576} {:answer/id #uuid "8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508592} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b2db0b-70aa-51ed-a1c5-b48983c58ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508592} :answer-field/id #uuid "0afcfbf6-047c-53c2-8a23-be6af1196c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131070" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-5,1)$\n\nII. $[0,5]$\n\nIII. $[5,10]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "bf9d0322-941e-5a54-b92e-f70ddfb5e71b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e532f7-a8d4-5ad0-87fa-e0abb07a53ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468010} {:answer/id #uuid "166b32ef-7d9c-5749-b3c9-7228a4faa2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468010} :answer-field/id #uuid "11346c34-25dc-5818-adc6-68489744c4a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468726} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcab39e9-589e-5993-9821-bf3f565fc3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468012} {:answer/id #uuid "5faf65eb-1420-5b19-84fa-7b4d080f51e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468013} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd1c1baa-2550-538a-9bce-29f2173a21b4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137588e4-2424-5d62-8fe6-5492472a9566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468014} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6eb95e-2a8c-5ac0-b216-c72fc2f4b9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468015} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cf26ba3-fa7e-5c2b-86e3-65a942cdda6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "122f233b-5382-5fef-8fbd-2b8682266e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-73481" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}?$   \n\nI. $[-1,5]$\n\nII. $[0,2]$\n\nIII. $[2,5]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not defined anywhere in the subinterval $[-1,0).$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The intervals $[0,2]$ and $[2,5]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[0,2]$  and $[2,5].$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "fb835e05-9455-5b93-ad9d-42052fd2df0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509003}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508384}]}
